
  <sn><md>### Environmental Transport

Environmental transport determines the `spatial distribution and movement` of toxicants across environmental compartments (air, water, soil, sediment, and biota):

![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-emon/QDoc/main/SES3/media/toxicology/toxicant_transportation.jpeg)

1. **Atmospheric Transport:** Volatile organic compounds (VOCs), gases (SO₂, NO₂), and particulate matter are ++dispersed++ by `wind` currents, ++entering biological++ receptors primarily via `inhalation`.

2. **Aquatic Transport:** Water-soluble contaminants migrate through `surface runoff`, `leaching` into groundwater, and `advective flow` in rivers and oceans. Aquatic partitioning depends heavily on `water solubility` and `suspended sediment binding`.

3. **Terrestrial &amp; Sediment Partitioning:** Hydrophobic toxicants adsorb tightly to soil organic carbon and sediment matrices, serving as long-term reservoirs for benthic and soil-dwelling organisms.</md></sn>
